.TH CHTTPCLIENT_DESTROY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chttpclient_destroy \- destroy an HTTP client and invalidate the handle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

chttpclient_destroy(cli)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro waits for every request that is in flight to finish. It
then frees every resource and sets cli to CHTTPCLI_INVALID.

"Every request that is in flight" covers all three tiers. It covers a
synchronous chttpclient_do or chttpclient_do_streaming call that still
runs on another thread. It also covers a request from
chttpclient_do_async, chttpclient_do_async_streaming,
chttpclient_do_pooled or chttpclient_do_pooled_streaming. Such a
request counts while the shared reactor engine still works on it. A
connection that sits idle in the pool does not count. This macro is
safe even when nothing waited yet on a ctpool_future from
chttpclient_do_async or from chttpclient_do_async_streaming.

cli must be a handle that is live now, or CHTTPCLI_INVALID, which is a
silent no-op. Three other kinds of value are a FATAL ERROR, and the
macro calls abort() and raises SIGABRT for them. This is safer than
the risk of a silent double free or of a use-after-free. The first is
a stale handle that an earlier call already destroyed. The second is a
stale handle that another thread destroys at this same moment. The
third is a forged value or garbage.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cli   An lvalue of the type chttpcli.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro evaluates cli exactly once. It must be a modifiable lvalue,
such as a variable or an element of an array, so an expression with a
side effect, such as handles[i++], destroys and clears one element.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_create_chttpclient (3),
.BR chttp_default_client (3)
